CAT = "iepsaEvents"; SEG = "EVT"
# QQI 4N20604 learning outcomes 2.3 (event security), 2.4 (crowd control), 2.10 (retail security guard
# and store detective), 2.11 (cash handling and movement in retail); Private Security Services Act
# 2004 s. 2 (definition of security guard, para (e)); Private Security (Licensing and Qualifications)
# Regulations 2022 (security guard (guarding) includes event work); Private Security (Identity Badge)
# Regulations 2024 Sch. 1.
Q = [
("A steward directs the flow of people into a concert for pay. Is this covered by the 2004 Act's definition of a security guard?",
 "Yes, it covers event work", ["No, stewards are exempt", "Only if inside a building", "Only if over 1,000 attend"],
 "Paragraph (e) of the Act's definition includes a person who controls, supervises, regulates, restricts or directs the movements of people at a place where an event is taking place or about to take place."),
("What is crowd density?",
 "People per area", ["How loud a crowd is", "How drunk a crowd is", "How old the crowd is"],
 "Density is the number of people in a given space, often measured per square metre. As it rises, people lose the ability to move freely and the risk of crushing grows."),
("A section of the crowd at the front of a stage is being pushed against the barrier. What should a steward do?",
 "Radio it in at once", ["Tell them to push back", "Climb the barrier", "Wait for the next song"],
 "Crowd pressure at a barrier can quickly become a crush. Report it at once so the control point can act, for example by stopping the show or easing the pressure."),
("Why should event staff watch the crowd rather than the performance?",
 "To spot trouble early", ["It is rude to watch", "Performers do not like it", "To count ticket holders"],
 "Facing the crowd lets staff see distress, fights, crushing, medical problems or someone in trouble early enough to act."),
("Which factor most affects how a crowd behaves?",
 "The type of event and mood", ["The colour of the barriers", "The day of the week", "The steward's height"],
 "A crowd's behaviour depends on the event, its mood, alcohol, how tightly packed it is, weather, delays and how well it is informed and managed."),
("Why is clear information, such as signs and announcements, important for crowd control?",
 "It reduces frustration", ["It makes the event louder", "It replaces all stewards", "It is needed for tickets"],
 "People who know where to go and why are less likely to push, panic or argue. Silence during delays breeds frustration."),
("Who normally has overall control of safety at a large event?",
 "The event safety officer", ["The first steward on duty", "The bar manager", "Any member of the public"],
 "Large events have a safety officer and an event control point that coordinate stewards, the emergency services and the organiser. Stewards report to them through their supervisor."),
("What does 'ingress' mean at an event?",
 "People entering", ["People leaving", "Queues at the bar", "Cars parking"],
 "Ingress is the arrival and entry of the crowd; egress is the departure. Both are peak times for crowd pressure."),
("At egress after a match, a gate is closed by mistake and people are backing up. What should a steward do?",
 "Radio for it to be opened", ["Tell people to climb over", "Leave by another exit", "Ask people to sing"],
 "A blocked exit during egress can cause a crush. Report it immediately on the radio so the gate can be opened and the flow managed."),
("How does a retail security guard differ from a store detective?",
 "Guard seen, detective covert", ["The guard needs no licence", "The detective has Garda powers", "They are the same role"],
 "A uniformed retail guard deters theft by being seen, while a store detective works in plain clothes to detect it. Both have only the powers any person has."),
("Why do shops keep only small amounts in tills and move the surplus to a safe?",
 "Less to lose in a robbery", ["Notes get creased in tills", "Tills cannot hold coins", "It speeds up refunds"],
 "Regular cash lifts to a safe keep the amount in any till low, so a robbery or till snatch takes less and the reward for trying is smaller."),
("What is the safest point at which to stop a suspected shoplifter, as good practice?",
 "Past the last payment point", ["When they pick up an item", "When they enter the shop", "When they look nervous"],
 "Good practice is to wait until the person has passed the last point of payment with unpaid goods, keeping them in view the whole time, so there is no doubt they meant not to pay."),
("A store detective loses sight of a suspect for a minute before she leaves. What should he do?",
 "Not stop her", ["Stop her anyway", "Search her bag", "Call her back loudly"],
 "If the suspect is out of view, she may have put the item back or paid. Good practice is not to stop someone after losing continuous observation."),
("Which is a common distraction technique used by shoplifters?",
 "One distracts, one steals", ["Paying with exact change", "Using a shopping basket", "Asking about prices"],
 "Thieves often work in pairs or groups: one distracts staff with questions or a scene while another takes goods."),
("Why should cash not be counted in view of customers?",
 "It tempts thieves", ["It slows the queue", "It is against tax law", "The till may jam"],
 "Counting cash in public shows how much is on site and where it is kept. Do it in a secure area away from view."),
("When moving takings to a safe in a store, which practice is best?",
 "Vary times and routes", ["Same time each day", "Announce it on the tannoy", "Use a clear bag"],
 "Predictable cash movements are easy to plan a robbery around. Vary times and routes, use a discreet container and, where the procedure says, go in pairs."),
("A robber demands the takings from a cashier while a guard is nearby. What is the priority?",
 "Safety of people, not cash", ["Protect the cash at all costs", "Tackle the robber", "Lock everyone inside"],
 "Lives matter more than property. Comply, observe and remember details for the Gardaí, raise the alarm when safe and preserve the scene."),
("What is a till 'float'?",
 "Opening change in the till", ["Cash taken by a thief", "A forged banknote", "The day's total profit"],
 "The float is a set amount of change placed in the till at opening, so cashiers can give change. It is counted in and out."),
("A guard is asked to check banknotes for fakes. Which is a sign of a genuine euro note?",
 "A changing hologram", ["Smooth, glossy paper", "Ink that rubs off", "No watermark at all"],
 "Genuine euro notes have raised print you can feel, a watermark and a hologram or emerald number that changes when tilted. Look, feel and tilt."),
("A queue for an event grows long and tempers rise. What helps most?",
 "Explain the delay", ["Close the gate", "Ignore all complaints", "Let people skip ahead"],
 "Telling people the reason for a delay and how long it may take calms a queue, as long as staff keep it moving fairly."),
("An event steward sees a person in the crowd who seems very drunk and unsteady. What should he do?",
 "Get welfare or medical help", ["Throw them out alone", "Give water, then leave", "Ignore it, it's common"],
 "Very drunk people are vulnerable to injury, crushing and assault. Call welfare or medical staff through control and keep them safe."),
("At an event, what should a steward do if a child is reported missing?",
 "Report it to control at once", ["Wait an hour first", "Search alone off site", "Tell the parent to wait"],
 "Report a missing child immediately so the control point can alert all stewards and secure exits. Take a description and stay with the parent or guardian."),
("Who should a retail guard call first if a suspect becomes violent after being stopped?",
 "The Gardaí on 112 or 999", ["The shop's head office", "The suspect's family", "A local newspaper"],
 "Violence is an emergency. Call the Gardaí, protect yourself and others, and use only reasonable force."),
("A steward at an event exit is asked to stop people re-entering without a pass-out. Which part of the guard definition covers this?",
 "Restricting movement", ["Monitoring security equipment", "Accompanying a guard dog", "Supervising other guards"],
 "Paragraph (e) of the Act's definition covers controlling, supervising, regulating, restricting or directing the movements of people at an event."),
]
